# Spring Boot Actuator — Health, Memory, Heap Dump, Threads, and Other Endpoints

Spring Boot Actuator itself is working. `DOWN` means that one or more health indicators (often a database or another external dependency) is reporting a failure.

1. First check the detailed health

In application.properties:

management.endpoint.health.show-details=always

Then call:

http://localhost:8082/actuator/health

You should get something like:

{
  "status": "DOWN",
  "components": {
    "db": {
      "status": "DOWN",
      "details": {
        "error": "..."
      }
    },
    "diskSpace": {
      "status": "UP"
    }
  }
}

2. If db is DOWN

This is very likely if you recently added Spring Boot JDBC / H2 / PostgreSQL.

For example, if you have:

implementation 'org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-starter-jdbc'
runtimeOnly 'com.h2database:h2'

Spring Boot automatically creates a database health check. If the datasource cannot connect, /actuator/health becomes DOWN.

Check your application.properties.

For H2, for example:

spring.datasource.url=jdbc:h2:mem:testdb
spring.datasource.driver-class-name=org.h2.Driver
spring.datasource.username=sa
spring.datasource.password=

3. If you don't need database health

If your application doesn't actually use a database yet, you can disable the DB health indicator:

management.health.db.enabled=false

Then:

http://localhost:8082/actuator/health

should report UP as long as the other health indicators are healthy.

Important:

Don't just disable the health check if your application needs the database. In that case, DOWN is useful because it tells you that the application cannot currently reach its database.


# Spring Boot Actuator — More Monitoring Data

Spring Boot Actuator can expose several useful endpoints for:

- Application memory
- JVM metrics
- Thread information
- Heap dump
- Spring beans
- Request mappings
- Environment/configuration
- Loggers
- Application health


1. Expose the endpoints

In application.properties:

# Expose Actuator endpoints
management.endpoints.web.exposure.include=health,info,metrics,loggers,threaddump,heapdump,mappings,env,beans

Then restart your application.

You can see the available endpoints at:

http://localhost:8082/actuator


2. Application memory / JVM memory

Use:

http://localhost:8082/actuator/metrics/jvm.memory.used

Other useful JVM memory metrics:

http://localhost:8082/actuator/metrics/jvm.memory.committed

http://localhost:8082/actuator/metrics/jvm.memory.max

You can also see the different memory areas.

Heap:

http://localhost:8082/actuator/metrics/jvm.memory.used?tag=area:heap

Non-heap:

http://localhost:8082/actuator/metrics/jvm.memory.used?tag=area:nonheap

For example, the response can contain:

{
  "name": "jvm.memory.used",
  "measurements": [
    {
      "statistic": "VALUE",
      "value": 1.2345678E8
    }
  ],
  "availableTags": []
}

The value is in bytes.


3. See all available metrics

Go to:

http://localhost:8082/actuator/metrics

You'll get metric names such as:

jvm.memory.used
jvm.memory.committed
jvm.memory.max
jvm.gc.live.data.size
jvm.gc.memory.allocated
jvm.threads.live
jvm.threads.daemon
process.cpu.usage
process.memory.used
system.cpu.usage

Then query an individual metric:

/actuator/metrics/{metric-name}

For example:

http://localhost:8082/actuator/metrics/jvm.threads.live


4. Heap dump

Actuator provides:

http://localhost:8082/actuator/heapdump

This is different from the metrics endpoint.

It generates a JVM heap dump (.hprof) that you can open with tools such as:

- IntelliJ IDEA
- Eclipse MAT
- VisualVM
- JDK Mission Control

The heap dump lets you investigate which objects are consuming memory.

For example:

Java Heap
 ├── byte[]
 ├── java.lang.String
 ├── HashMap
 ├── Customer
 ├── Employee
 └── ...


5. Thread dump

Use:

http://localhost:8082/actuator/threaddump

This is useful for finding:

- Blocked threads
- Waiting threads
- Deadlocks
- Thread pools
- Running threads


6. Application beans

To see the Spring beans:

http://localhost:8082/actuator/beans

For example:

SampleController
DataSource
JdbcTemplate
HikariDataSource
...


7. Application configuration / environment

http://localhost:8082/actuator/env

This shows Spring's environment and property sources.

Be careful with this endpoint because it can expose configuration information. Don't expose it publicly in production without proper security.


8. Request mappings

To see all your controller mappings:

http://localhost:8082/actuator/mappings

For example:

GET /status
GET /actuator/health
GET /customers
POST /customers


9. Log levels

You can inspect logger configuration:

http://localhost:8082/actuator/loggers

And a particular logger:

http://localhost:8082/actuator/loggers/org.springframework.web


10. Useful Actuator configuration for development

For your current local Spring Boot application:

# Actuator
management.endpoints.web.exposure.include=health,info,metrics,loggers,threaddump,heapdump,mappings,beans,env

# Show health details
management.endpoint.health.show-details=always


Then your main URLs are:

Purpose                         Endpoint

Overall health                  /actuator/health
All metrics                    /actuator/metrics
Heap used                      /actuator/metrics/jvm.memory.used
Heap committed                 /actuator/metrics/jvm.memory.committed
Maximum heap                   /actuator/metrics/jvm.memory.max
Threads                        /actuator/metrics/jvm.threads.live
CPU                            /actuator/metrics/process.cpu.usage
Heap dump                      /actuator/heapdump
Thread dump                    /actuator/threaddump
Spring beans                   /actuator/beans
Environment                    /actuator/env
URL mappings                   /actuator/mappings
Loggers                        /actuator/loggers


Most important endpoints for memory troubleshooting:

/actuator/metrics/jvm.memory.used
/actuator/metrics/jvm.memory.max
/actuator/heapdump

Metrics tell you how much memory is being used.

Heap dump helps you investigate what is using that memory.